Optional modern appearance
==========================

Configuration / interface / miscellaneous contains:
  Use modern flat appearance (restart required)

It defaults to off. Save the setting and restart emelFM2 to use it. Changing
the setting does not interrupt the current session, including running file
operations and terminals. Other preference changes and window rebuilds keep
the appearance selected at startup. Turn the option off and restart to return
to the original GTK appearance. No other saved settings are rewritten.

The modern appearance flattens control surfaces and uses consistent symbolic
icons for recognized built-in actions. It preserves the selected GTK palette,
including dark backgrounds, text, and selection colors. File-type colors,
output tags, ANSI terminal colors, user fonts and custom icon directories are
unchanged. Explicit icon paths and unrecognized plugin/user icons retain their
normal lookup. If SVG loading is unavailable, existing icons are used instead.
Symbolic icons retain each original image's actual dimensions.

The same native widgets, action identifiers, layouts, column sizing, toolbar
placements, keyboard/mouse bindings and confirmation dialogs are used. There
is no new sidebar, header bar, theme selector or file-operation code path.
GTK 3 uses local CSS providers for control surfaces, without color or size
overrides. GTK 2 uses a flat style engine with the resolved theme palettes and
metrics. GTK 2 combo-box arrow buttons and file column headers retain their
native styles to preserve private spacing and theme-specific dark backgrounds.
Highlighted GTK 2 menu items use the theme's selection background, including
themes whose normal highlight is painted from artwork instead of a hover color.
Window decorations remain desktop-managed.

Build and packaging
-------------------

WITH_MODERN_UI ?= 1 is the default in Makefile.config and debian/rules. Including
support does not enable the appearance in user profiles. Both GTK 2 and GTK 3,
with or without VTE, support the setting. No new linked library is required.

Use WITH_MODERN_UI=0 for a binary without the presentation module or embedded
symbolic artwork. The configuration checkbox is omitted, and a modern-ui=true
value from another build is safely retained as an unknown option. The compiled
capability is reported by --build. Feature changes participate in the existing
build signature so switching the flag rebuilds the relevant objects.

Pass the same flags when building and installing. For example:
  make WITH_GTK2=1 WITH_MODERN_UI=1
  make install WITH_GTK2=1 WITH_MODERN_UI=1 PREFIX=/your/staging/root/usr

The artwork is embedded in the optional module. There is no separate asset
directory to install or remove. UI.md and docs/ui-concept/ are design materials,
not runtime resources, and are not installed by the package.

Validation
----------

After building with the matching GTK generation:
  GTK_PACKAGE=gtk+-2.0 tests/run-modern-ui.sh
  GTK_PACKAGE=gtk+-3.0 tests/run-modern-ui.sh

These tests compare resolved palettes and widget allocations in light and dark
themes with the preference off/on, check custom colors/icons, and verify that
menu highlights remain visible for pointer and keyboard navigation and the
preference remains fixed through window rebuilds. Run them with both build
flag values to verify the omitted-capability path too. The tests use temporary
profiles and a virtual display, leaving the user's configuration untouched.

Existing content-drag, pane-tabs, application-smoke and VTE UI tests also accept
E2_TEST_MODERN_UI=true to exercise the same interactions with modern styling.
E2_MODERN_TEST_THEMES can select additional installed themes for palette and
geometry comparisons, for example E2_MODERN_TEST_THEMES=Matcha-dark-sea.
